;;; A positive derived under `(absent (q one))`, and a refutation that reads
;;; it beside `(q one)`: a program that is not monotone.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;; ── The program ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `(p one)` is derived at root because `(q one)` is absent. `p` and `q`
;;; together are a contradiction. The goal is `q`, so the rung proposes
;;; `(q one)`.
;;;
;;; ── What a node is, here ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Committing `(q one)` falsifies `derive-p`'s guard, but nothing retracts
;;; (docs/ein/reasoning/02-kb-evolution/03-absent-and-naf.md § What is not
;;; provided), so a fork of root holds the `(p one)` root derived beside the
;;; `(q one)` it commits: a stale fact.
;;;
;;; Does a guard's `absent` premise bind the state the search ends in? Ein
;;; does not decide it: a node is a fork of root, an assumption on a program
;;; the check flags (Q-M1e2.2). The readings:
;;;
;;;   guards are premises   committing `(q one)` withdraws `(p one)`, and the
;;;                         clash never fires.
;;;   append-only           the fork holds both, and the clash fires.
;;;
;;; The program is stratified (`q` is no rule's conclusion).
;;;
;;; ── What is said of it ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `p-and-q-clash` reads `(p ?x)`, which `derive-p` derives under
;;; `(absent (q ?x))`, and `q` is a relation a commitment can extend;
;;; unfolded it is `(thing ?x) (absent (q ?x)) (q ?x) ⟹ (false)`. The static
;;; check flags the program at `derive-p`'s `absent`. It loads, every
;;; command answers it with the warning that it is not monotone, and nothing
;;; is claimed of its answer (Q-M1f.564). The file states no claim.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
(relation thing T :why "{?3} is a thing")
(relation p T :why "p holds of {?3}")
(relation q T :why "q holds of {?3}")
(thing one)
(rule derive-p ()
  :match  (and (thing ?x) (absent (q ?x)))
  :assert (p ?x) :why "{?x} is a thing and no q fact holds of it, so p holds of {?x}")
(rule p-and-q-clash ()
  :match  (and (p ?x) (q ?x))
  :assert (false) :why "both p and q hold of {?x}, which cannot be")
(query :goal (q ?x))
